WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.399
A runaway trolley.

2
00:00:02.649 --> 00:00:05.027
Five people in its path.

3
00:00:05.277 --> 00:00:07.271
You could save them all…

4
00:00:07.371 --> 00:00:09.639
…by killing someone else.

5
00:00:11.339 --> 00:00:13.330
The Trolley Problem.

6
00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:22.848
In 1967, the philosopher Philippa Foot imagined the driver of a runaway trolley.

7
00:00:23.548 --> 00:00:27.111
Ahead, five people are working on the track.

8
00:00:27.461 --> 00:00:30.856
On a side track, there is just one.

9
00:00:31.556 --> 00:00:36.075
Foot thought the answer was clear. Whatever he does, the driver kills.

10
00:00:36.375 --> 00:00:41.040
So, “without hesitation,” he should steer for the less occupied track.

11
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.942
Then Judith Jarvis Thomson changed one detail.

12
00:00:48.342 --> 00:00:50.545
The driver has fainted.

13
00:00:50.845 --> 00:00:54.289
You are a bystander, standing next to a switch.

14
00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.248
If you do nothing, the trolley will kill the five.

15
00:01:00.048 --> 00:01:06.049
If you pull the lever, it will turn onto the side track, and kill the one.

16
00:01:06.749 --> 00:01:09.104
Should you pull the lever?

17
00:01:17.104 --> 00:01:20.831
Most people say yes. But notice what changed.

18
00:01:21.331 --> 00:01:25.134
The driver chose between killing one, and killing five.

19
00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:29.228
You choose between killing one, and letting five die.

20
00:01:29.728 --> 00:01:33.695
And doing harm usually seems worse than merely allowing it.

21
00:01:34.395 --> 00:01:37.396
So why is pulling the lever permissible?

22
00:01:40.396 --> 00:01:42.411
Thomson's first answer:

23
00:01:42.711 --> 00:01:47.254
you don't create a new threat. You redirect one that already exists,

24
00:01:47.454 --> 00:01:50.576
away from five people, toward one.

25
00:01:53.576 --> 00:01:57.172
But in 2008, she changed her mind.

26
00:01:57.572 --> 00:02:01.348
Imagine a third track, where you yourself are standing.

27
00:02:01.748 --> 00:02:05.167
Few of us would turn the trolley onto ourselves.

28
00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:10.844
And if you wouldn't, Thomson argued, you have no right to turn it onto a stranger.

29
00:02:11.244 --> 00:02:15.364
That would be like stealing someone's wallet to give to charity.

30
00:02:18.364 --> 00:02:21.667
So, would you pull the lever? And why?

31
00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:29.279
This is the trolley problem in its simplest form. Philosophers have since built many harder variations,

32
00:02:29.279 --> 00:02:33.410
like pushing a stranger off a footbridge to stop the trolley.

33
00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.353
The scenario is deliberately artificial.

34
00:02:37.653 --> 00:02:43.087
Strip a moral choice down to its bare bones, and it shows us what we really value.
